son" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-94215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+Bt{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha +(\\alpha t+\\beta )=t+2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha t+\\beta -2\\alpha =t+2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ \\beta -2\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =4,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t+4.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-94215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f2bb605cbc955863ea35929a9765891d741a632975e12a521b9245804cc593d7"} {"question" "q-94215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-94215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-94215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-94210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-94210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-7y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and sinusoidal forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{{e}^{7t}}+B{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-\\alpha +6\\beta -7\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\beta -6\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{25}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{4}{25},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-94210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/ques