" "question/problem" "value" "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=12\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-64957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+6y=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha t+\\beta )=12t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha t+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=12t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =\\frac{5}{3},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-64957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f2bb605cbc955863ea35929a9765891d741a632975e12a521b9245804cc593d7"} {"question" "q-64957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-64957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "q-64957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/state.json" "file_sha256" "b542de9399ae4b8634b13ff99341acbc2577be697eed926d16a073d1c36176f5"} {"question" "e-6633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\nt^2 y'' - 4ty' + 6y = 2t+6, \\quad t > 0\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(t) =  At^3 + Bt^2,$ find the particular solution $y_p(t)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/example-6633.json" "file_sha256" "466220772a031b2c02eac3d9aa56d79cf21a637fc20bc8f0575a81b76e1fdb0e"} {"question" "e-6633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2t+6.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\ny_p(t) = \\alpha t + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' =  \\alpha,\n\\qquad y_p'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}(0)-4t(\\alpha )+6(\\alpha t+\\beta ) & =2t+6 \\\\ (-4\\alpha +6\\alpha )t+6\\beta  & =2t+6 \\\\ 2\\alpha t+6\\beta  & =2t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,t) \\\\ 6\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = 1$ and $\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = t + 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/example-6633.json" "file_sha256" "466220772a031b2c02eac3d9aa56d79cf21a637fc20bc8f0575a81b76e1fdb0e"} {"question" "e-6634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/5dec2a9790065a32f95712885a67f7893f3f323f0c342bc19848686f1680ad47.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/example-6634.json" "file_sha256" "f412bf84dbf86161777399