ure-workers/differential/14071621/assets/3e2eec56a0a2eb6590fe59c5d8e8fc9c588b17de53a266c6719329b0e7841f9d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/ec53de44465314f6b2450484a70c2da859b48f3efec8659e400af26b16396553.png) Therefore, the complementary solution ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/34d64ed13215f9b9612c3b53db483a999913f843c4d58427f3d2bfe65ebe0d9d.png) is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/ef693a5bc51357c04c56b71e0a1aa74910701650fdf4c812b3ac8b02b0cf1a56.png)\n\nNow, we find the particular solution. The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/c00b6908be18de1a5bd0a6cd6a607a8cb6dc71ee650efe1777f739e3206180ca.png) contains only an ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/7ab7f3b150a249d735cc23143a45b74dc49ab199a8fb1cb29cc100d8b966c165.png) term, so we assume that the particular solution is also of the form \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/c127d18ff3ff0c783d08b6a0530702e9865b8f4e3b761e424c6029b16893e081.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/e0eb5c67e8a631dbd986d1d3ad160b0c1d4e2167a367ac8b1bd00021b9b1c75f.png) is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/e1cab0ad0f363d8b4799c169e93d41c965c7990851dd2258c92251a70bc13e31.png) gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/75a2fb41037a14a29f29a9b1855ee6b80c20b10edfd483f2308b4ac91997a80e.png)\n\nTo find the value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/ded6bb167d2956277fd322b71231b47617e0f507bab0c03ef6e8e4215ce509d1.png) we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/a46095fc5d883600f17d036890aacd3d414f0620bb6f7135c507dd810e905320.png)\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/e4305cb136272705bd3c1b940ab1169d9d4097a2a8e0361830b158e9161bf6d9.png)\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/442345edd17b8167b1607389107526de7a359044a5ee29752b775eb62543aa0f.png)\n\nand the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/assets/c3331d87acc601da747055eb76b4a96bb7e33c1e284da02bf60a3f839fc9e0d1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/example-6634.json" "file_sha256" "f412bf84dbf861617773994cb8a1bbb5d5529e6e46b4312a39a6765aacb631d2"} {"question" "e-6635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nt^2 y'' +2ty' = t^2 e ^ {t}, \\quad t>0\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(t) =  \\dfrac{A}{t} + B,$ find the particular solution $y_p(t)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/example-6635.json" "file_sha256" "b28aeba6259b87d1b4a88db8545bbe77f5f81af450f7040dae15c8bcbc8e347f"} {"question" "e-6635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side is not a polynomial, so we need to use another solution technique. Here, we are given the complementary solution $y_c(t) =  \\dfrac{A}{t} + B,$ which means that we have two fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(t) = \\dfrac{1}{t}  , \\qquad y_2(t) = 1.\n$$\n\nBecause we have two fundamental solutions, we can use variation of parameters.\n\nTo use variation of parameters, we must first reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\ny'' + P(t) y' +Q(t) y = f(t).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we divide both sides of the given equation by $t^2,$ and get\n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{2}{t}y'  =  e ^ {t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\ny_1(t) = \\dfrac{1}{t}  , \\qquad y_2(t) = 1, \\qquad f(t) = e^t.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t})(1)^{′}-{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(t)$ and $y_2(t)$ are independent.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, a particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =-\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{2}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\int {t}^{2}{e}^{t}=-({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}, \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int t{e}^{t}=(t-1){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}}{t}+(t-1){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{2}{t}){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/example-6635.json" "file_sha256" "b28aeba6259b87d1b4a88db8545bbe77f5f81af450f7040dae15c8bcbc8e347f"} {"question" "e-6636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\nx^2y''+xy'   + y =  \\ln x, \\quad x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/example-6636.json" "file_sha256" "d354809abe8fc41db35fd669337c0d74f538e3f2dd03c8f5065c21bbef8f51a5"} {"question" "e-6636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x=e^t,$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x=e^t,$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = e^t = x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac1x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+y=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous ODE equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing.\n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A\\cos t+B\\sin t.\n$$\n\nSince right-hand side of the inhomogeneous ODE is a polynomial of degree $1,$ we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+ \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\alpha\n,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}t^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0+(\\alpha t+\\beta)=t\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of [math]t[/math]}) \\\\ \\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $\\alpha=1$ and $\\beta=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = t.\n$$\n\nNow, all that remains is to write the above solution in terms of the original variable $x.$ Since $x=e^t,$ we have $t = \\ln x,$ and therefore\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =\\ln x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071621/example-6636.json" "file_sha256" "d354809abe8fc41db35fd669337c0d74f538e3f2dd03c8f5065c21bbef8f51a5"}] "usage" {"q-64891" [] "e-6633" [] "e-6636" [] "q-64943" [] "e-6634" [] "q-64871" [] "q-64933" [] "q-64878" [] "e-6635" []} "basis" 2567 "retractions" [[17592186152546 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} - 4xy^{′} + 6y =-6x + 1, x > 0$\n\nhas the complimentary function $y_{c}(x) = Ax^{2} + Bx^{3}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation."] [17592186152545 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} - 4y = 6x, x > 0$."] [17592186152539 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} - 2xy^{′} + 2y = 2x^{3}, x > 0$."] [17592186152543 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 2y = t^{3}e^{t}, t > 0$\nhas the complimentary function $y_{c}(t) = \\frac{A}{t} + Bt^{2}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(t)$ to this equation.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n$∫t^{3}e^{t}dt = (t^{3} - 3t^{2} + 6t - 6)e^{t} + C$"] [17592186152541 :question/problem "Find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ of the equation\n$x^{2}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 3x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 4y = 4\\ln x, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x = e^{t}$.*"] [17592186151896 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$t^2 y'' - 4ty' + 6y = 2t+6, \\quad t > 0$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(t) = At^3 + Bt^2$, find the particular solution $y_p(t)$ to this equation."] [17592186151896 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2t+6$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = \\alpha t + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = \\alpha, \\qquad y_p'' = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2}(0) - 4t(α) + 6(αt + β) &= 2t + 6 \\\\\n(-4α + 6α)t + 6β &= 2t + 6 \\\\\n2αt + 6β &= 2t + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{2α = 2\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients oft) \\\\ 6β = 6\\end{vmatrix}(equating the constants)\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = 1$ and $\\beta =1$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = t + 1\n$$"] [17592186151897 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' - y = 3x^2, \\quad x>0$."] [17592186151897 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complimentary function $y_c(x)$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\nx^2y'' + xy' - y = 0\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} - x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + λx^{λ} - x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + λ - 1] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + λ - 1 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)(λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}+ c_2 x\n$$\n\nNow, we find the particular solution. The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation $(3x^2)$ contains only an $x^2$ term, so we assume that the particular solution is also of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha x^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =2 \\alpha x, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 2 \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}(2α) + x(2αx) - αx^{2} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\n(2α + 2α - α)x^{2} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\n3αx &= 3x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get $\\alpha = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =x^2\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{x} + c_{2}x + x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151898 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$t^2 y'' +2ty' = t^2 e ^ {t}, \\quad t>0$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(t) = \\dfrac{A}{t} + B$, find the particular solution $y_p(t)$ to this equation."] [17592186151898 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side is not a polynomial, so we need to use another solution technique. Here, we are given the complementary solution\n\n$$\ny_c(t) = \\dfrac{A}{t} + B\n$$\n\nwhich means that we have two fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(t) = \\dfrac{1}{t}, \\qquad y_2(t) = 1\n$$\n\nBecause we have two fundamental solutions, we can use variation of parameters.\n\nTo use variation of parameters, we must first reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\ny'' + P(t) y' +Q(t) y = f(t)\n$$\n\nTo do this, we divide both sides of the given equation by $t^2$, and get\n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{2}{t}y' = e ^ {t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\ny_1(t) = \\dfrac{1}{t}, \\qquad y_2(t) = 1, \\qquad f(t) = e^t\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{t})(1)^{′} - (\\frac{1}{t})^{′}(1) \\\\\n&= 0 + \\frac{1}{t^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(t)$ and $y_2(t)$ are independent.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, a particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(t) &= y_{1}(t)u(t) + y_{2}(t)v(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= - ∫(f(t)y_{2}(t))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dt = - ∫t^{2}e^{t} = - (t^{2} - 2t + 2)e^{t}, \\\\\nv(t) &= ∫(f(t)y_{1}(t))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dt = ∫te^{t} = (t - 1)e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(t) &= y_{1}(t)u(t) + y_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\\n&= - ((t^{2} - 2t + 2)e^{t})/(t) + (t - 1)e^{t} \\\\\n&= (1 - \\frac{2}{t})e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151899 :question/problem "Find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ of the equation\n$x^2y''+xy' + y = \\ln x, \\quad x>0$."] [17592186151899 :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x=e^t$, we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x=e^t$, we note that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = e^t = x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{dt}\\frac{dt}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x}\\frac{dy}{dt}, \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{dy}{dt}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} + \\frac{1}{x} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dt})_(⏟)_(product rule) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{dy}{dt} + \\frac{1}{x} \\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx}\\frac{d}{dt}_(⏟)_(chain rule)(\\frac{dy}{dt}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{dy}{dt} + \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dt^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dt^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{dy}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dt^{2}} + y = t\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous ODE equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing.\n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A\\cos t+B\\sin t\n$$\n\nSince right-hand side of the inhomogeneous ODE is a polynomial of degree $1$, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1$, i.e.\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+ \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t)$:\n\n$$\n0+(\\alpha t+\\beta)=t\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{α = 1\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of [math]t[/math]) \\\\ β = 0\\end{vmatrix}(equating the constants)\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $\\alpha=1$ and $\\beta=0$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n$y_p(t) = t$.\n\nNow, all that remains is to write the above solution in terms of the original variable $x$. Since $x=e^t$, we have $t = \\ln x$, and therefore\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =\\ln x\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186150145 17592186150146 17592186150147 17592186150148 17592186150149 17592186150157 17592186150158 17592186150159 17592186150160 17592186150161 17592186150169 17592186150170 17592186150171 17592186150172 17592186150173 17592186150181 17592186150182 17592186150183 17592186150184 17592186150185 17592186150187 17592186150188 17592186150189 17592186150190 17592186150191] "immutable_fields" [[17592186150144 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150144 :answer-field/id] [17592186150144 :answer-field/key] [17592186150144 :answer-field/type] [17592186150